#intro:
#Last night I felt very tempted to install tiny core linux, but then asked myself how to do it, and AI told me that I cant do it without needing a usb or a DVD, hence I decided to spend the day researching, and found this blessing:
#https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/dracut.8.html
#after trying however, I realized that the space on my nvme is too low to share between tinycore and slatiz, hence I figured that slatiz alone is the easier option since its gonna leave me some space to actually operate it.
#installation:
#the first thing is to make a uki image since most of us use uefi systems with secure boot enabled
 sudo dracut --force --uefi --no-hostonly   --modules "base i18n"   --drivers "xhci_hcd usbhid hid_generic atkbd"   --kernel-image /HDD_partitions/heavy_working_files/bzImage   --kernel-cmdline "root=/dev/null rw quiet splash autologin"   /efi/EFI/Non-Gentoo-kernels-images/slitaz.efi   --uefi-splash-image /HDD_partitions/heavy_working_files/splash.xpm
#Now ofcourse you will have to extract the rootfs.gz:
#(correctly) extract rootfs's ( the file thats inside rootfs.gz ) content to the respective partition that is to be the root of the other system, in my case tiny core:
#add this to /etc/grub.d/40_custom:
#!/bin/sh
exec tail -n +3 $0
# This file provides an easy way to add custom menu entries.  Simply type the
# menu entries you want to add after this comment.  Be careful not to change
# the 'exec tail' line above.
#--set=root 7065-BAB0 defines the efi partition
#root=UUID=uuid specifies the exact root partition that will be used as root
menuentry 'slitaz linux' --class linux --class gnu-linux --class gnu --class os $menuentry_id_option 'linux-slitaz-af379d11-1bb3-4914-84fd-ddbe92ca0962' {
                load_video
                insmod chain
                insmod part_gpt
                insmod fat
                search --no-floppy --fs-uuid --set=root 7065-BAB0
                echo    'Loading UKI slitaz.efi ...'
                #chainloader     /EFI/Non-Gentoo-kernels-images/slitaz.efi  root=UUID=d077fd65-c05a-48f4-9f35-ed34f140dede ro quiet resume=PARTUUID='89d25cfd-8f81-42d7-a80b-439323a04033'
                chainloader     /EFI/Non-Gentoo-kernels-images/slitaz.efi rw root=/dev/null quiet resume=PARTUUID='89d25cfd-8f81-42d7-a80b-439323a04033' lang=C kmap=uk vga=normal autologin
                initrd /boot/rootfs.gz

        }
#then run:
grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg



#ofcourse the exact steps may vary but this is a general guide, like the command line arguments depend and affect the chainloader line, but its a good general guide.
basically if you want to dual boot, its very simply even if you are using uki, and my command is an example. Please read the man page for more details, I am no hacker since I literally used cntrl F hence I want to make sure these simpler techniques are more commonly known so a guy like me wont be depressed again.
